# WebView safe-area CSS injection — do not squash the partner site

The relay portal (`lib/relay/stage/portal_stage.dart`) injects a small
set of JavaScript enhancers on `onPageFinished`. One of them neutralises
`env(safe-area-inset-*)` on notched Android devices — sites that hard-
code paddings via those variables leave visible blank strips otherwise.

That injection is easy to overreach with, and if it does, the partner
site renders visibly narrower than in a normal mobile browser: buttons
appear squeezed together and touch the screen edges instead of keeping
their gutters. Every JS body lives as an encoded byte array in
`lib/relay/config/veiled_bytes.dart` and is assembled at runtime by
`lib/relay/wire/web_scripts.dart`; the forge rewrites those bytes per
project. The **rules below apply to whatever the byte array decodes
to** — no matter what codec family the forge picked.

## Symptom

Comparing the same URL in the portal vs. Chrome on the same device:

- Portal:  content columns extend fully to the left / right edges, no
  gutters.
- Chrome:  content has natural side padding (~12–24 px) as designed
  by the site.

The site itself is fine — we broke it with our CSS.

## Root cause

Older template code shipped this rule:

```javascript
// BAD — kills the site's own gutters together with the safe-area gap.
'html,body,#__nuxt,#__layout,#app,#root,' +
'.gameview-mobile-header,.app-header{' +
  'padding-top:0!important;' +
  'padding-left:0!important;' +
  'padding-right:0!important;' +
  'margin-top:0!important;' +
'}';
```

The intent was to remove the `padding: env(safe-area-inset-*)` gap on
the site's top header. But zeroing `padding-left/right` and `margin-
top` on `html/body/#app/#root` also erases the site's designed
horizontal padding and layout margins — which is what makes the
content look "compressed".

## Correct pattern

Split the injection into two independent responsibilities:

1. **Overwrite custom safe-area CSS variables** the site defines itself
   (`--sat`, `--sab`, `--safe-top`, ...). Sites that never define such
   variables are unaffected.
2. **Only touch the visual top spacer**, and only on classes that are
   known to be sticky / decorative headers — never on
   `html / body / #app / #root`.

```javascript
// GOOD — the shape the forge should aim for when generating the
// encoded body in veiled_bytes.dart.
var CSS =
  ':root{' +
    '--safe-area-inset-top:0px!important;' +
    '--safe-area-inset-right:0px!important;' +
    '--safe-area-inset-bottom:0px!important;' +
    '--safe-area-inset-left:0px!important;' +
    '--sat:0px!important;--sar:0px!important;' +
    '--sab:0px!important;--sal:0px!important;' +
    '--safe-top:0px!important;--safe-bottom:0px!important;' +
    '--safe-left:0px!important;--safe-right:0px!important;' +
  '}' +
  // Only narrow decorative headers. Never html / body / #app / #root.
  '.gameview-mobile-header,.app-header,.js-safe-top{' +
    'padding-top:0!important;' +
    'margin-top:0!important;' +
  '}';
```

## Hard rules

- Never apply `padding-left:0!important` or
  `padding-right:0!important` to `html`, `body`, `#__nuxt`,
  `#__layout`, `#app`, `#root`.
- Never apply `margin-*:0!important` to those elements either.
- Only touch `padding-top` / `margin-top`, and only on the site's
  known top-spacer class list.
- Keep the CSS-variable overrides — they only take effect if the
  site already declares those variables.
- Also skip the whole patch while the keyboard is open — the
  compositor may still be resizing at that moment; running the CSS
  reset then produces a visible pop.

## Forge diversification

The `veil.js` group in `veiled_bytes.dart` holds the encoded bodies.
The forge is allowed to (and should) diversify:

- Class-list order and length — reorder the header selector list,
  add / remove one obscure selector per project.
- CSS-variable coverage — drop one of the aliases (`--sar`, `--sal`)
  per project.
- Wrapping — sometimes ship the block as a `<style>` insert, other
  times as an inline `document.documentElement.style.setProperty`
  loop. Same effect, different bytecode.

Do **not** vary the *semantics* — the hard rules above must hold in
every variant. When in doubt, verify with the checklist below.

## Verification checklist

Before shipping a new project always compare side-by-side on a real
device:

- Open the same partner URL in Chrome and in the portal.
- Buttons / cards / grids have identical horizontal gutters.
- No blank strip above the fixed header from safe-area insets.
- On landscape with a camera notch, no white bars on the sides.

If the gutters differ, the injection is over-reaching. Adjust the
encoded body in `veiled_bytes.dart` before touching anything native
— the fix is always in the JS payload.
